1 INTRODUCTION
Driving simulators as a tool during automotive development and research have several benefits compared to using an actual vehicle. The simulated environment can be kept consistent between test runs, which allows for refined tests where only the explanatory variables are varied. Driving simulators also allow for scenarios that are too dangerous to perform in a real environment or scenarios that involve complex interactions between actors that would be almost impossible to orchestrate in real life.
