Another challenge with driving simulators is to limit the amount of simulator sickness induced by the simulator. Simulator sickness can occur when there is conflicting sensory input from the visual and vestibular systems [28]. This conflict can originate from delays in the simulator’s imaging system, as well as from the inability of the simulator motion system to reproduce the correct motion feedback. When starting to feel the onset of simulator sickness, the driver may involuntarily adapt their driving behavior to alleviate the symptoms. This adaptation can lead to questionable experimental validity. Previous research has shown that display systems’ performance affects the level of simulator sickness [11].
The most common display system for small simulators uses one or more monitors placed in front of the driver. Monitors are very cost effective, although it is impossible to cover a large field-of-view without interruption by the screen bezels. If an uninterrupted field-of-view is desired, then the standard solution is to utilize projectorbased solutions where multiple projectors are combined to give a large, high-resolution view. The most modern systems reach resolutions that are getting close to the limits of the performance of the human eye [31].
The most common use of virtual reality in the automotive sector has been as a tool for design evaluation [3]. In this context, it is essential to have high-resolution images to make the correct design decisions. Thus the most common display solution has been to use CAVE solutions [17], since they can provide higher resolutions than the current generation of head-mounted displays.
Head-mounted displays have not been commonly used, since they have a history of suffering from low resolution, low field-of-view, high tracking latency, and high price. The focus of the image inside a head-mounted display is at a fixed distance, which may result in Vergence-Accommodation Conflict [19]. In turn, this conflict can result in headaches and fatigue for the user [20].
During recent years, the technological development of head-mounted displays has surged, with improved tracking performance, higher resolution displays, and wider field-of-view. As a result, the question of replacing computer screens and projectors as the primary solution for presenting the virtual environment to the users has reappeared. The main appeal with this is the possibility of increased realism and lower cost of investment compared to traditional display solutions.
2 RELATED WORK
An early study by Schill et al. [32] describes how Daimler-Benz evaluated a head-mounted display inside their dynamic driving simulator. They performed a between-group study (n = 48), where they compared the difference between projectors and head-mounted display. They compared tasks involving speed perception, lane-change maneuvers, and a car following task. They found similar driving behavior for the performed tasks, and the subjectively rated simulator sickness in the two groups was also similar.
Burns and Saluäär [5] compared real driving with driving in a static simulator equipped with a head-mounted display in a within-group study (n = 11). The researchers compared speed perception, time to complete turns, lane-keeping as well as subjective workload. They also recorded the visual behavior via cameras inside the head-mounted display as well as inside the vehicle. Their results showed that the subjects used similar times to complete turns in both studied conditions. However, they had slower speed and worse lane-keeping after turning when they wore the head-mounted display. Their visual behavior was also affected, and they needed longer glance durations before turning compared to real driving. In addition, the subjects rated a higher required mental workload when using the head-mounted display.
Coates et al. [7] integrated a head-mounted display with a wide field-of-view (120° horizontal and 67° vertical) in the Renault dynamic simulator. The authors point out the need for a wide horizontal field-of-view for speed perception and a wide vertical field-of-view to see both the environment and the dashboard at the same time.
A study by Carlozzi et al. [6] investigated the possibility of using driving simulators for training subjects with spinal cord injuries to drive. Their study compared a head-mounted display with a three-monitor setup for a
