simple static driving simulator in a between-group study (n = 54). Their results show less lateral control for the group using the head-mounted display solution, but they saw no significant difference in motion sickness rate between the two groups.
Grabe et al. [13] compared a projection screen with a head-mounted display without head-tracking. They used a within-group study to test with both 90° and 45° field-of-view while the drivers (n = 12) performed a slalom course in a motion-base simulator. The deviation from an ideal path was measured. The field-of-view angle had no significant effect, but the head-mounted display had a greater error for each tested condition. The head-mounted display solution performed worse even when the researcher used the maximum available field-ofview in the head-mounted display, which was greater than the available field-of-view for the projection screen. Their results suggest that the selected slalom task is more dependent on central vision. The authors also saw no significant difference when the motion system of the simulator was disabled. The angular resolution, brightness, and contrast were superior in the head-mounted display compared to the projection screen, suggesting that none of these factors can explain why the drivers performed worse while wearing the head-mounted display.
Aykent et al. [1] performed a within-group study where the subjects (n = 14) made a subjective comparison between a head-mounted display and a three-monitor solution in a fixed-base simulator. Their results favored the monitor-based solution due to the low field-of-view and high motion sickness in the head-mounted display, while the subjective level of presence was scored equivalently for both compared setups.
In a similar fixed-base driving simulator study, Colombet et al. [8] compared a head-mounted display to a foursided CAVE virtual reality room. The subjects (n = 12 + 12) were instructed to drive two different scenarios, one with several changes in longitudinal motions (acceleration/deceleration) and one that had several turns creating yaw rotations. The subjects performed the two scenarios at both low and high speeds. The results showed that the head-mounted display users were more susceptible to motion sickness during fast longitudinal motions. For yaw rotational motions, the rate of simulator sickness was similar regardless of the chosen display technology.
Weidner et al. [36] also used a static simulator to compare traditional projectors, active stereo projectors, and a head-mounted display in a between-group study (n = 94). The driving task was to perform a lane-change maneuver. The study found no significant effects on measured driving behavior. They saw an increase in simulator sickness with the head-mounted display, although the difference was only significant when comparing the active stereo projector and the head-mounted display.
A recent study by Zöller et al. [37] performed a comparison between projectors and head-mounted displays in a static simulator. The study, which used a within-group design (n = 41), also incorporated a mono-camera to the head-mounted display to show the actual interior of the vehicle and the drivers’ own hands. They measured the difference in immersion, speed-perception, distance estimation, visual acuity, and reaction time during a simulated rural road scenario. The results showed no significant difference in immersion or distance estimation. The speed-perception task resulted in under-estimation of own speed when driving with a head-mounted display compared to an over-estimation for projectors. The visual acuity test showed that the subjects were able to identify road signs much earlier with projectors compared to the head-mounted display (mean distance 79 m compared to 24 m). The head-mounted display case also showed slower reaction times. During the post-study interview, the subjects stated some physical discomfort of using the head-mounted display, and a majority showed a preference for the projector-based graphics. Hartfiel and Stark [15] investigated if introducing a midsize hexapod motion system might alleviate the motion sickness experienced when driving while wearing a head-mounted display with either the motion system enabled or disabled. The subjects (n = 100) drove 4 minutes in one of three scenarios; highway driving, rural driving, or urban driving. The results hint at a small reduction in motion sickness when using a motion system but showed no statistically significant effect.
An alternative to using a motion system is to use a simulated environment inside an actual vehicle on a test track [12]. The main benefit of this setup is that there is no need for any vehicle dynamics simulation, and the motion feedback from the vehicle is correct by default. Using this type of setup, Benz et al. [2] compared the
