3 FOCUS OF CURRENT RESEARCH
If head-mounted displays are to be used in driving simulator applications, then there is a need to know if the technology in itself affects driving behavior. Previous research has mainly focused on general driving either in highway, rural, or urban environments. This study focused on finding more specific answers to why different display types may elicit particular responses in the subjects, hence targeting the study toward specific driving tasks. Thus, the goal was to find maneuvers where the response was different depending on the display type and find which maneuvers are unaffected.
Previous meta-analysis [4] have indicated problems with head-mounted displays in scenarios that require any of the following conditions:
Fast side-to-side head motions. Users of head-mounted displays are susceptible to latency when doing rapid head turns [16]. The reason being that any latency will introduce a phase error in the orientational tracking, which becomes particularly noticeable when the direction of the head-movement changes.
Significant lateral motion of the vehicle. Any swift side-to-side movement of the vehicle performed by the driver will cause the entire scene to move across the retina in a similar way to head rotations, thereby evoking the same problematic behavior as above. For simulators, this motion needs to be replicated by the motion system, and any discrepancies between the visual feedback and motion feedback are likely to increase motion sickness.
High visual acuity. Most countries define a legal requirement for corrected visual acuity to be considered fit to drive. In the EU, the minimum requirement is a binocular visual acuity of at least 0.5 [33]. This level of acuity roughly translates into an angular display resolution of 30 pixels/°. This can be compared to the current generation of head-mounted displays, which ranges from 10 to 15 pixels/°.
Interaction with physical hardware inside the vehicle cabin. When putting on a head-mounted display, the user blocks out all but the virtual environment. To be immersed entirely can be beneficial in some situations, but it can be problematic if the user is supposed to interact with real hardware in the real environment. The head-mounted display also blocks out the users’ own body, which can be problematic. This lack of self-embodiment forces the users to rely solely on proprioception to determine the position of their own hands and may cause sensory miscues. There are ways around this problem using hand-tracking and replicating the real hardware as objects in the virtual world [22].
There are also potential benefits of using head-mounted displays. Drivers in simulators tend to use longer safety distances compared to the real-world due to a reduction in speed perception. Speed perception may improve from having motion parallax due to the dynamic changes of the viewpoint caused by the changes in the driver’s head position [26].
The dynamic viewpoint combined with the binocular display technology enables more depth cues in the near to medium range (below 30 m) in a head-mounted display compared to projectors or monitors [29]. These added depth cues may enable the driver to make better estimates of the position and orientation of their vehicle.
