4 METHOD The study presented in this article used a within-group design with two conditions; One with projector-based graphics and one using a head-mounted display. The visual display type conditions were balanced between the subjects, where half of the subjects started driving the task with projector-based graphics, and the other half started with a head-mounted display. The balanced design was used to reveal any order effects between the studied conditions, which was especially important given that the amount of simulator sickness may accumulate from one condition to the next.
4.1 Apparatus The study utilized the VTI Simulator 3 [25], which has a motion system with 4 degrees of freedom. The foundation of this motion system is a linear system spanning 7.5 m (see Figure 1(a)). This linear system can achieve accelerations up to 8.0 m/s2 and provide realistic lateral acceleration feedback for high dynamic maneuvers. On top of the linear system is the projection dome, which can be articulated with hydraulics to provide tilt coordination, i.e., pitch and roll of the dome. Tilt coordination can be used to provide simulated accelerations in both lateral and longitudinal directions. The vibration table inside the dome offers 4 degrees of freedom, i.e., heave, surge, roll, and pitch. This vibration table is used to give realistic high-frequency feedback representing the road surface contact and suspension forces. The front half of a real SAAB 9–3 cabin is attached to the vibration table (see Figure 1(b)).
The virtual test track used in this study consists of two straight segments connected by two hairpin curves. The primary motivation of using a test track designed in this fashion was to facilitate a future repeat study of the same maneuvers but on a real test track. Consequently, the dimensions had to be kept within the available road surface at the intended proving ground. The two hairpin curves were selected so that their radius matched the smallest roundabouts recommended in Sweden. However, this road design had consequences for the motion cueing strategy in the simulator.
The simulator employs a motion cueing strategy that uses a linear motion system to match the lateral lane position. This strategy results in realistic side-to-side acceleration for momentary maneuvers. For longitudinal acceleration and sustained lateral acceleration, the simulator uses pitch and roll motion. The narrow radius of the hairpin curves exceeded the valid range of the motion cueing algorithm, which resulted in incorrect roll motion in the simulator. Since the remaining parts of the virtual roads contain few parts that use the roll motion, the decision was made to disable the rolling motion. Regretfully this meant disabling the pitch motion as well. Hence the motion feedback relied solely on the vibration table and the lateral linear system.
