4.1.1 Projector System. The projector system uses six Sony HW30 SXRD-projectors with FullHD resolution. The projectors are positioned in a 3-by-2 configuration with a combined horizontal field-of-view of 115° seen from the driver’s seat. This results in average angular resolution of 42.7 pixels/°. The image update frequency for the projectors is 60 Hz.
4.1.2 Head-Mounted Display. There are particular factors to consider when deciding which head-mounted display to use inside a moving vehicle. The head-mounted display’s weight must be kept as low as possible, as a heavy device can cause neck-strain during high dynamic maneuvers. A very bulky device may also limit the user’s motion as such a device will collide with the cabin interior. The available tracking system must also work inside the relatively small vehicle cabin, either with the device’s built-in tracking system or the ability to integrate with a suitable third-party tracker.
The head-mounted display selected for the study was an Oculus Rift CV1, which has an angular resolution of 13.8 pixels/°(0.23 pixels/arcmin [21]) in the center of the visual field. This angular resolution was well in line with other head-mounted displays available on the open market when the study was performed [23]. The image update frequency for the Oculus Rift is 90 Hz, which was among the highest available at the time of the study [30]. The field-of-view inside the head-mounted display is 94° horizontal and 93° vertical. There were devices with wider field-of-view available on the market, but these devices were bulkier and had higher weight, lower angular resolution, and lower update frequency [24].
The Oculus Rift CV1 uses an inertial-optical tracking system. The inertial part measures the linear and rotational accelerations of the headset. These measurements are integrated twice to calculate the current position and orientation. The drawback of this integration is that any noise in the sampled measurements will accumulate and cause drift in position and orientation. Consequently, there is a need for an optical part (which Oculus has named the constellation tracking system), which uses two or more fixed cameras placed around the user. The optical system uses computationally expensive image processing, but it can operate at a lower update rate, since its primary purpose is to correct for drift caused by the inertial system. However, the Oculus Rift CV1 is not designed for use on a dynamic platform, and any motion of the dynamic platform gets incorrectly interpreted by the inertial system as head movements instead. The optical tracking system could, in theory, correct for this, but that would require placing the tracking cameras outside of the moving platform. This camera placement is impossible in this case, since the simulator is covered and has a much broader envelope than the conceivable tracking area for the Oculus tracking cameras. In theory, it should be possible to subtract the motion information from the simulator motion system to compensate for its contribution to the inertial information inside the headmounted display [10]. Regrettably, the motion tracking algorithms of the Oculus Rift are closed source, which makes this type of compensation hard to implement. Consequently, the built-in tracking system was disabled, replacing it with a third-party tracker, in this case the OptiTrack V120, which uses two cameras operating at 120 Hz and tracks the position of a group of reflective markers placed on the object to ascertain its position and orientation.
4.1.3 Virtual Environment. To perform the study, there was a need for a virtual environment that featured a test track whose geometry allowed for the desired maneuvers. This track has the following layout; The starting point is a straight road with two lanes each 3.5 m wide (see Figure 2). Outside these lanes, there is a 1.5 m verge. The straight part is 680 m long and has a decline of 0.5 %. After the straight road section, the right lane narrows over 100 m until only the left lane remains. The road then makes a 180° turn with an outer radius of 11 m. After this turn, the road widens over 100 m to two lanes again. A new straight section of 680 m follows, which has an incline of 0.5%. The straight section is followed by yet another narrowing section and a 180° turn, which finally widens and reconnects with the starting point.
The road was built by hand using the OpenDrive logical description and then converted to a visual environment using in-house image generation software tools. The visual environment was exported from this tool,
