4.2 Driving Tasks Five different driving tasks were selected to highlight the differences between projector-based display systems and head-mounted displays. Each of the selected tasks is described in detail below, together with each task’s evaluation criteria.
4.2.1 Speed Perception. The subjects were asked to drive this task with the speedometer and tachometer disabled to investigate if head-mounted displays improve speed perception. The subjects started from standstill and were asked to accelerate up to what they perceived to be 40 km/h. As soon as they felt they had reached the instructed speed, they had been instructed to press a button on the steering wheel.
When they pressed the button, they were instructed to decrease their speed to 20 km/h. Once again, pressing the button when they felt they had reached the instructed speed.
The task then repeated with 40 and 20 km/h in the same fashion. The driven speed was recorded to analyze the deviation compared to the instructed reference speed. Previous research has shown that increased field-ofview results in better speed perception [9]. The head-mounted display allows the user to turn the head for a more immersive experience. Additional depth cues from the binocular display system, coupled with the motion parallax from head motion, should allow for more depth cues than projection-based graphics [26]. Thus the hypothesis is that the deviation would be smaller for head-mounted displays.
