4.2.3 Medium Speed Lane Change. The medium speed lane change task used the same type of lane change setup, the difference being that the subjects were instructed to drive in 40 km/h. The idea was to force the subject to make fast lateral movements with the vehicle, which previous research indicated could be hard to do using a head-mounted display.
The task used the same measurements as the low-speed lane change task. Each subject repeated the medium speed lane-changing maneuver twice before starting the next task. In this case, the hypothesis is that driving with a head-mounted display would have more pronounced deviations and more steering wheel reversals compared to driving with projector-based graphics.

4.2.4 Sign Detection. The subject performed a sign detection task to examine the effects of the reduced visual acuity in head-mounted displays. The task started with the subjects positioned with the vehicle positioned on the centerline of the road. A roadsign appeared on the centerline of the road far away in front of the vehicle. This roadsign could have one of two possible symbols (see Figure 4), selected randomly. The subjects were instructed to drive toward the roadsign in 40 km/h. As soon as they could distinctly identify the symbol on the sign, their task was to steer and pass on the side indicated by the sign. The subjects performed four of these maneuvers per display type.
An individual with perfect vision should be able to discern objects extending 1 arcmin [35], which corresponds to a width of the identifiable features of 0.58 mm at 2-m distance. The selected road sign is designed to be used for testing visual acuity in a vehicle driving context. The width of the black bars on the symbol and the white distance between them are 58 mm. Thus, if viewed in real life and assuming perfect vision, then the symbol should not be possible to decipher until being closer than 200 m. The projector system used in this study have a resolution of 42.7 pixels/°. At least 2 pixels are needed to form a distinguishable feature, and thus the narrowest object that a individual with perfect version can discern extends 2.81 arcmin (see Equation (2)). Given the resolution of the projector-based setup used in this study, the theoretical limit is 71.0 m (see Equation (3)). The same calculation

