4.2.5 Narrow Curve Maneuver. The virtual test track contains a hairpin curve at each end to evaluate how well tight curves in urban environments are handled. The subjects were instructed to make a smooth turn with the vehicle centered in the available lane. The average deviation from an ideal path in the center of the lane was measured, as well as the number of steering wheel reversals needed to maneuver through each curve. The hypothesis is that the subject would benefit from having the added depth cues in the head-mounted display and the ability to turn around to see the full environment. These benefits should enable the subjects to drive the curve with smaller deviations and fewer steering wheel reversals.

4.3 Subjects
Twenty-five subjects (13 men and 12 women, mean age 40 years, range 21–65 years) were recruited from the general public via an open recruitment form on the institute’s website. Subjects were required to have a valid driver’s license and to be able to drive without using glasses, although wearing contact lenses was permitted. Glasses were eliminated due to space constraints inside the head-mounted display. Individuals who self-reported using medication that affected their balance were excluded from participation. Women who self-reported being pregnant were also excluded. All signed an informed consent form before participating in the study. All subjects in the study received two cinema tickets as a reward for their participation.

4.4 Procedure
Before the subjects drove the simulator, they read a written instruction of all the driving tasks they would be asked to perform. The test leader then administrated a pre-drive simulator sickness questionnaire (SSQ) [18] to record a pre-test baseline. The subjects were then accompanied to the vehicle in the simulator and shown how to adjust the seat and cabin temperature for optimal comfort. When the subjects used a head-mounted

