display, they were instructed how to adjust the interpupillary distance setting to their individual preference before putting it on. They were also shown how to adjust the headbands for a firm and comfortable fit.
Both conditions started with one lap around the virtual test track to familiarize the subjects with the simulator. During this lap, they were instructed to try out different speeds and changing lanes. The average time for completion of this familiarization sequence was 2 min 21.3 s (standard deviation 17.6 s). After the familiarization lap, they were instructed to stop before starting the real driving task. Before each task, the test leader repeated the instructions of the given task to the subject.
Once the subjects had completed all driving tasks of the first test condition, they were escorted from the simulator. The subjects responded to a post-test simulator sickness questionnaire as well as a questionnaire regarding perceived realism. This realism questionnaire used a Likert-scale with seven steps where the subjects were asked to rate the realism for factors such as longitudinal and lateral motion, as well as the visual and auditorial environment. Once the questionnaires were answered, the subjects were allowed to rest for five minutes before being accompanied back to the simulator to drive the second condition.
The average time to complete all driving tasks for a given display type condition was 10 min 26.1 s (standard deviation 1 min 6.6 s).
When the subjects had completed the second condition, they performed another post-test simulator sickness questionnaire and a realism questionnaire.
5 RESULTS Three persons (one man and two women) had to abort the study due to severe motion sickness. One subject failed to perform most of the maneuvers as instructed, resulting in unusable data for this subject. Thus this subject is excluded from the analysis as well. Consequently, the analysis of driving behavior does not contain data from these four individuals.
5.1 Analysis of Driving Behavior Analysis of variance was used to investigate the effects of the visual display types, the variation between subjects, and the interaction between these two factors. The visual display type was used as a fixed factor, while the subject was used as a random factor. Since the study used a within-group design, the conditions were performed in balanced order, and the order number was used as a fixed factor. In the speed perception analysis, the reference speed was used as a fixed factor.
A model selection was made for each of the response variables. Visual display type and subject was forced to be in the model even if they were not significant, while order number, reference speed (if present), and all interactions were allowed to be removed in the model selection. Each factor and each two-factor interaction were included in a preliminary model. Non-significant interactions were then removed. Non-significant main variables other than those forced to be in the model were removed unless they were part of a significant interaction.
The results of the analysis of visual display types, the difference between projectors and head-mounted displays are presented in Table 1 and discussed in detail in the following sections.
Significant variation between subjects can be seen for all measurements, except for the average deviation in the low-speed lane change task and the sign detection task (see Table 2).
The interaction effect between the visual display type (projectors vs. head-mounted display) and subject also revealed that different subjects react differently to changing between projectors and head-mounted displays (see Table 3), except for the steering wheel reversals in the medium speed lane change task.
