5.2 Simulator Sickness Simulator sickness may accumulate over time, and the short break between the two conditions was probably not enough to eliminate such potential accumulation effects from the previous condition. However, the balanced design used in this study would reveal such ordering effects, and the analysis of the simulator sickness showed no significance for the ordering of the conditions.
The subjects who chose to abort due to severe motion sickness did so after driving only one display type condition. Therefore including them in the statistical analysis would skew the results in favor of the omitted display type. The recorded results in the simulator sickness questionnaire for the subjects who chose to abort are presented in Table 5.
Analysis of variance was used to study the effect of the visual display types and the variation between the remaining subjects on motion sickness and its sub-scales. The analysis showed no significant differences between the two visual display types for the resulting simulator sickness, neither for the total SSQ-score nor for any sub-scales (see Figure 9). The differences between individual subjects are more substantial than any difference between the display types.
5.2.1 Adaptation. An increase in simulator sickness can lead to behavioral adaptation as the subjects try to alleviate or minimize further increase. This adaptation can result in the subjects changing either their driving behavior, visual behavior, or both.
Analysis of the head movement of the individuals with the highest SSQ scores for the oculomotor subscale shows a clear difference in head-turning behavior compared to the group with the lowest SSQ scores (see Figure 10). This analysis suggests that one possible adaptation in behavior is a reduction in head-turning when subjects begin to experience oculomotor symptoms of motion sickness.
5.3 Perceived Realism The analysis showed no statistically significant differences between the two display types in any posed questions in the realism questionnaire.
