6 DISCUSSION This study shows that it is possible to use a head-mounted display as the primary display in a dynamic driving simulator. The observed changes in lateral and longitudinal driver behavior are minor when comparing projectors and a head-mounted display.
The lateral driving behavior seems mostly unaffected by the use of a head-mounted display. A significant difference can be seen in the most extreme curve in the tested scenario, which might support the use of a headmounted display for scenarios that involve tight curves, such as city driving. There is a non-significant trend in favor of projectors for the average lateral deviation, which would be consistent with previous research [13]. A possible explanation for this effect is a conflict with the Vestibulo-ocular reflex induced by system latency [27]. This reflex is triggered by head motion and makes the eyes rotate to keep the image of the environment fixed on the retina even though the head is moving. This reflex can cause conflicts when using a head-mounted display, since the reflex’s compensatory actions introduce errors as the display is fixed relative to the head [16].
The longitudinal behavior also displays minor changes between the two studied display types. Previous research has shown that the perceived speed is higher than the reference speed when the horizontal field-of-view is large, and vice versa [34]. The available horizontal field-of-view in the head-mounted display used in this study is narrower than the field-of-view made available by the projectors. The users can rotate their heads to compensate for this difference. However, the speed perception task results suggest that the underestimation of speed in the head-mounted display may be a result of the lower field-of-view. Another factor that may impact these results is the lack of motion feedback in the longitudinal direction, although this affected both studied conditions.
The most substantial difference between the two imaging systems can be found in the sign detection task. This difference is not unexpected considering the technical specifications regarding available angular resolution. Nevertheless, special consideration must be taken to remedy the effect of the comparably low resolution in the current generation head-mounted displays. One possible way to remedy the low resolution in the head-mounted display is to scale up roadsigns to let drivers detect them at more realistic distances [37]. Although, just scaling up roadsigns might lead to unrealistic environments, which might break immersion. Scaling up other objects in the world would probably result in an even greater breaking of immersion. It could also lead to difficulties in estimating self-motion due to changes in the optical flow [14].
