Sign detection is not the only task where high angular resolution is needed. Any type of target detection can benefit from having realistic sight distances, such as scenarios including overtaking, complex interaction with multiple actors, and detection of vulnerable road users. Thus an increase in angular resolution is highly desirable for the driving simulation use case.
In previous driving simulator research, the connection is unclear between introducing a head-mounted display and an increase in simulator sickness. However, most studies that report a large increase in simulator sickness were performed using static simulators [1, 8, 36, 37]. Regardless, there are studies performed in static simulators with non-significant differences as well [6]. For comparison, few studies explore the difference in a dynamic driving simulator. The early study by Schill et al. [32] showed no significant difference regarding simulator sickness. The study by Hartfiel and Stark [15] compared head-mounted displays in a dynamic simulator with the motion enabled and disabled. Enabling the motion in their case resulted in a small reduction in simulator sickness, but the effect was not significant. Similarly, this study could not identify any difference in simulator sickness between the two display types, which may be attributed to the motion system. Another reason might be that the scenario was relatively short, and a more prolonged scenario might have induced more simulator sickness.
6.1 Limitations and Future Work The selected scenarios did not involve interaction with specific hardware inside the cabin, except for the steering wheel and pedals. Hence no hand tracking was used in this study, and the subjects could not see their own hands when they drove the simulator wearing the head-mounted display. Neither were any other representations of the driver’s body present in the virtual world. This lack of self-embodiment was something that some of the subjects commented on during the post-drive questionnaire.
The study consisted of selected specific maneuvers, and it would be interesting to perform a similar study but instead use an extended sequence of more naturalistic driving.
It would also be desirable to investigate the effects of having a higher angular resolution, but that would be dependent on hardware evolution.
Another aspect that needs to be considered is the ergonomic effects of wearing a head-mounted display. The scenario used in this study is relatively short, but the added weight of the head-mounted display might be a considerable problem if the subjects were to drive a much longer scenario. Other ergonomic factors might also come into play during extended use; for example, the lenses may fog up due to increased temperature and humidity inside the head-mounted display.
